## The asserts return type

A normal function's return type describes the value it gives back. An assertion function instead uses an `asserts` predicate to describe what is true about the program if the call returns at all:

```ts
function assert(condition: unknown, msg?: string): asserts condition {
  if (!condition) throw new Error(msg ?? 'Assertion failed');
}
```

After `assert(x)`, the compiler treats `x` as truthy for the rest of the scope — because if it were not, the function would have thrown.

## Asserting a specific type

`asserts v is T` narrows to a concrete type:

```ts
function assertIsString(v: unknown): asserts v is string {
  if (typeof v !== 'string') throw new Error('Expected string');
}

function handle(input: unknown) {
  assertIsString(input);
  input.toUpperCase(); // input: string from here on
}
```

## Assertion functions vs type guards

A type guard returns a `boolean` and you branch on it; an assertion function returns `void` and narrows by not throwing:

```ts
// guard — you handle the false branch
function isString(v: unknown): v is string { return typeof v === 'string'; }

// assertion — the false branch throws for you
function assertIsString(v: unknown): asserts v is string { /* ... */ }
```

Use a guard when the negative case is normal control flow; use an assertion when a failure is genuinely exceptional — invariant checks, or narrowing after a runtime validation.

## A caveat

The `asserts` signature must be written explicitly — TypeScript will not infer it. And the body has to actually throw on the failure path, or the assertion is a lie the compiler will still believe.

